        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> First of all, is there a reason for using custom autocommand instead of default `MiniSessions.config.autowrite`? It does [almost exactly that](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/ee7c02297cf95cba07f8bf8da3ecf99337a1c15e/lua/mini/sessions.lua#L81).\r\n\r\nI wanted to have a session that is always what I was working on last. I still use autowrite to write if I'm already in a session (like if I were in a project session, it would save, but it would also save it as the last session I'm working on). This way, I don't have to worry about resuming my work if I forget to save the session when I'm not in a session atm.\r\n\r\nAlso I've seen that this happens with all plugins that have \"special buffer usage\". Nvim-Tree, Neogit, and other plugins that provide context aware/special buffers often break upon restoration. But they don't garble up the layout nearly as nvim-notify. I think this is just an inherent flaw with mksession. It would be amazing if there was a way to make it work with nvim-notify but I'm definitely doing swell with just disabling the verbose options and making sure I don't save sessions when a notification shows.\r\n\r\nThanks for your hard work!",
